From 5e05c38793cdbb26059d7a53b61127f2c11f78be Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 12:32:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 09/14] feat(deploy): let Prometheus scrape the metrics
 endpoints on Kubernetes

Both services now serve /metrics without authentication, but on
Kubernetes nothing could scrape the control plane: the default
policies only let the gateway reach port 8080, and the gateway does
not forward /metrics. The gateway's /metrics shares the open port 8081
with the API and the probes, and the manifests did not say what an
Ingress should expose.

monitoring/network-policy.yaml is an optional pair of policies that
lets pods labelled app.kubernetes.io/name=prometheus in the monitoring
namespace reach 8080 on the control plane and 8081 on the gateway.
Both selectors are meant to be edited for the actual Prometheus; the
gateway rule only matters once the gateway's ingress is narrowed.

The README states that the endpoints serve unauthenticated aggregate
data, that a NetworkPolicy opens the whole port (these pods can also
call the control plane API directly, still with a key), and that an
Ingress should only route /api/v1/. It also gives a scrape
configuration that scrapes each pod rather than the Service, so the
counters of different gateway replicas are not mixed. The manifest
headers point to the new file.

Validated on kind with Calico 3.33 in nftables mode. Without the file
the control plane's /metrics times out from a Prometheus-labelled pod;
with it that pod gets the metrics, while an unlabelled pod in the
monitoring namespace and a Prometheus-labelled pod in another
namespace still time out. With the gateway's ingress narrowed to an
ingress controller namespace, only the Prometheus pod and the ingress
controller reach port 8081. Prometheus 3.15 running the README scrape
configuration with only the documented RBAC scrapes both pods.
